{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>But if he stands a day or two days.</b> If Scripture had mentioned 'a day' alone, we would have understood a day of Torah, which is night and day, like the day of Creation, concerning which it is written (Genesis 1): ״ויהי ערב ויהי בקר״. But it added יומים, meaning a day that is like two days — and the intent is that one takes from two days, which is מעת לעת (from time to time). And since at first it said ״ומת תחת ידו״, which implies that he dies at the time of the striking immediately, it returns to clarify: if he stood on his feet on the day of the striking, or even if he could not stand at all on that day but stood on the following day, the master is exempt; but if he did not stand at all, he is liable even though he died on the second day, for ״ומת תחת ידו״ we call it. And it did not mention standing on the third day, for once he lived three days he is exempt, since we do not call it ״ומת תחת ידו׳." "<b>And one may ask</b> why Scripture did not mention here the law of a father who strikes his son a cruel blow and he dies under his hand — what is his law and what is his punishment. And the answer is that it is not a common thing that a father would intend to strike his son a deadly blow; but a master whom a slave provokes in a grave matter and strikes him a blow that does not subside — he intends to kill him. But if he stands a day or two days, since his outcome proves regarding his beginning that his intention was not to kill him, he shall not be punished, ״כי כספו הוא״ — for he is his money, and a person does not intend to lose his property and to kill his slave, who is the acquisition of his money."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:41.301374796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}